The Learning Studio

**Digital Learning Pyramid Resource Hub — Product Blueprint v6**

*17/07/26 v6  |  Synthesis: positioning, design language, and the v5 review fixes  |  Build brief for Gemini + GitHub*

# 1. Executive Summary — v6 Position

v6 does not introduce new scope. It closes the gap between "safely in your lane" and "insanely useful" by making explicit something that has been true since v1 but never stated cleanly: digital practice exists in service of teaching, learning and assessment — it strengthens how staff deliver TLA, without ever defining what that TLA practice should be. Section 3 states this as a formal position, not an aside.

Everything flagged in the last review is fixed here: four staff-situation rows reframed from pedagogy to digital tools, one row cut outright, the WalkThrus reference removed, framework alignment moved to a controlled vocabulary, an automated schema validator added to governance rather than relying on written rules alone, and all five V1 cards — not two — built to prove the discovery model.

This version also answers a question nobody had asked yet: what does "beautiful" actually mean here, in specifics a builder can implement, not adjectives a builder has to interpret. Section 10 gives Gemini an accessible, verified colour palette, a named accessible typeface, and concrete layout rules — because "calm and modern" was never buildable, and this project's own governance rule is that accessibility must be tested, not eyeballed. That rule now applies to beauty as well.

# 2. What Changed From v5, and Why

| **Change** | **Why** | **What it fixes** |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Added a formal "digital support for TLA, not TLA ownership" position (Section 3) | The tension between staying in lane and being genuinely useful was being managed implicitly, row by row, rather than stated as a principle | Gives every future card and route a single test to write against, instead of relying on case-by-case scope judgement |
| Reframed four staff-situation rows from pedagogy language to digital-tool language | "Hinge points," "reduce teacher dependency" and "lasting learning" are TLA concepts, not digital ones, even when the resolution is a digital tool | Keeps the discovery layer inside the digital remit at the language level, not just the routing level |
| Cut "How do I evidence practical work without overcomplicating it?" outright | This one touched assessment evidence sufficiency, which is EQA/awarding-body territory in FE — a regulatory risk, not just a taste risk | Removes the one situation with genuine compliance exposure rather than softening it |
| Removed the WalkThrus reference entirely | WalkThrus is a commercial, trademarked product whose value is the step-card format itself — the same format this product uses for its own cards | Removes a stylistic-imitation risk that Rosenshine, UDL and QFT don't carry, since none of those are sold as a card format |
| Converted framework_alignment to a controlled vocabulary | Freeform text let Gemini generate plausible-sounding but unverified framework claims | Removes the highest-risk hallucination surface in the schema |
| Added an automated schema validator to Section 13, not just written AI rules | "Gemini must never guess" is intent, not enforcement | Catches broken card references and schema drift on every commit, not on the next human review |
| All five V1 cards must prove the discovery model, not two | A V1 where only 40% of cards demonstrate the actual differentiator doesn't prove the concept, it dilutes it | Makes the V1 pitch honest about what it demonstrates |
| Added a named, accessible design language (Section 10) | "Calm, modern, staff-friendly" gave a builder nothing to implement or test against | Gives Gemini a verified colour palette, a named accessible typeface, and testable layout rules |

# 3. Position: Digital Support for TLA, Not TLA Ownership

**This is the sentence that resolves the tension in every review so far, and it should be reproduced wherever the product is described to anyone outside the immediate build team.**

| **The Learning Studio does** | **The Learning Studio does not** |
| --- | --- |
| Strengthen how staff deliver existing teaching, learning and assessment practice, through digital, accessible and inclusive means | Define, prescribe or judge what that teaching, learning or assessment practice should be |
| Translate a real staff situation into a digital possibility and an accessible resource | Translate a real staff situation into a pedagogical instruction or assessment method |
| Reference established frameworks (Rosenshine, UDL, QFT) to explain why a digital practice works | Claim ownership, authorship or institutional endorsement of those frameworks |
| Make the case, through demonstrated usefulness, for what a wider platform could become | Propose, launch or govern that wider platform without separate institutional sponsorship |

*Every future card and Finder route can be tested against this table in seconds: if the answer to a staff situation is a tool, a setting, a template or an accessible format, it belongs here. If the answer is a teaching decision, an assessment judgement, or an evidence methodology, it belongs to someone else.*

# 4. Product Identity

| **Item** | **Definition** |
| --- | --- |
| Working name | The Learning Studio |
| Product type | A problem-to-practice digital resource hub aligned to the Digital Learning Pyramid, in service of wider TLA practice without owning it. |
| Primary user promise | Tell me what you're trying to do. I'll show you a digital, accessible way to do it — beautifully, and fast. |
| Design personality | See Section 10 for the buildable specification. In one line: quietly confident, never busy, accessible as the aesthetic rather than a constraint on it. |
| What it is | A guided discovery hub for digital practice, accessibility, inclusion, assistive technology and AI-supported practice, that strengthens TLA delivery without defining TLA strategy. |
| What it is not | Not a whole-college TLA system, not a staff capability assessment, not a replacement for Jisc Discovery, not a system storing personal data. |

# 5. Authority and Scope Boundary

| **In scope — current digital remit** | **Out of scope — separate ownership** |
| --- | --- |
| Digital Foundations, Digital Inclusion and Digital Innovation content, guidance and resource routing, in service of existing TLA delivery | Defining, prescribing or judging whole-college teaching, learning or assessment strategy |
| Situation-first discovery from real staff language to digital, accessible resources | Any staff capability self-assessment — Jisc Discovery's role, not duplicated here |
| Reference-only alignment to public frameworks (Rosenshine, UDL, QFT, DigCompEdu, WCAG) | Any commercial or trademarked framework product whose value is a specific format (e.g. WalkThrus) — reference the underlying concept via its open source instead |
| Aggregate, non-identifying usefulness signals and dated, reviewed content | Any capture, storage or export of individual staff or learner data |
| Demonstrating a reusable pattern that could inform a future institutionally owned platform | Owning, proposing or pre-architecting that wider platform without separate sponsorship |

# 6. Pyramid Alignment Map

| **Pyramid layer** | **What lives here** | **Source / status** |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Digital Foundations | Core M365 and digital-skills capability content | Digital Skills Framework — signed off by Ben Manning and Neil Davies |
| Digital Inclusion | Accessibility practice, assistive technology, Learning Without Barriers | Accessible by Design and Learning Without Barriers — existing content, first full layer for V1 |
| Digital Innovation | AI-supported and emerging digital practice | Pedagogy Playbook — existing, reframed around digital practice specifically |
| Cross-cutting thread | Learning Without Barriers tagged across all three layers | Existing content, re-tagged rather than duplicated |

# 7. Problem-to-Practice Discovery Architecture

| **Stage** | **What the user sees** | **What the system does** |
| --- | --- | --- |
| 1. Staff situation | "What are you trying to do?" | Captures the problem in teacher language. |
| 2. Underlying digital need | Lightly shown as "You might be trying to…" | Maps the situation to a digital capability gap — never a pedagogical diagnosis. |
| 3. Digital possibilities | "Here are some ways digital could help." | Suggests no-tech, low-tech, digital and AI-supported routes. |
| 4. Digital Practice Card | A polished, shareable card, built to the Section 10 design spec. | Steps, why it works, accessibility and inclusion lenses, framework alignment, downloads. |
| 5. Resource action | Download, watch, search, try, adapt. | Moves staff into action without needing the taxonomy first. |

# 8. Refined Staff Situation Bank

Rewritten so every underlying need names a digital capability gap, not a pedagogical concept. One situation from v5 is removed entirely rather than softened.

| **Staff question** | **Underlying digital need** | **Digital discovery routes** |
| --- | --- | --- |
| How do I catch up absent learners? | Capture and share learning digitally for later access | Teams recap post; photo capture; short screen/audio explanation; accessible summary; retrieval questions from lesson notes |
| How do I show learners what I'm thinking? | Make expert thinking visible and reusable | Visualiser; screen recording; OneNote annotation; annotated slides; short think-aloud video |
| How do I capture live modelling? | Record and reuse a demonstration digitally | Document camera/visualiser; phone/tablet video; Stream; Clipchamp; Teams post |
| How do I run a quick digital check for understanding? | A fast, low-friction digital capture of responses | Mini whiteboards; Plickers; Forms; polls; exit ticket; photo/voice evidence |
| How do I give learners digital tools to work more independently? | Reduce reliance on repeated live explanation through reusable digital resources | Checklists; how-to videos; Teams channels; structured FAQs; AI-generated learner checklists |
| How do I support reading and writing barriers? | Digital access and assistive technology for text | Immersive Reader; Read&Write; dictation; captions; text-to-speech; accessible Word layout |
| How do I make resources accessible? | WCAG-aware structure and assistive-technology compatibility | Headings; alt text; captions; colour contrast; Accessibility Checker; accessible templates |
| How do I reduce repeated explanations? | Reusable digital explanation that learners can return to | Short videos; Teams FAQs; recap posts; annotated screenshots; step-by-step guides |
| How do I capture practical work digitally? | Digital capture of a process or product for later reference | Photo evidence; video snippets; Teams assignment upload; voice notes |

*"**How do I evidence practical work without overcomplicating it?**"** is removed. Digital capture of practical work is in scope; judging what counts as sufficient assessment evidence is not, and the two were too close together in the original wording.*

# 9. Platform-Wide Learning Lenses

| **Lens** | **Boundary** |
| --- | --- |
| Accessibility | Non-negotiable standard on every card — not optional advice. |
| Inclusive practice | Digital inclusion route, not a wider SEND policy replacement. |
| UDL | Supporting lens, not a full UDL training programme. |
| Trauma-informed | Practical link only — reduces uncertainty, avoids public exposure — not clinical guidance. |
| Quality First Teaching | Contextual alignment only — shows how digital practice supports QFT, does not claim ownership of QFT strategy. |
| Evidence-informed | References Rosenshine, UDL or other open, public-domain research only. No trademarked formats, no proprietary card systems. |

# 10. Design Language — Beautiful and Accessible by Construction

Accessibility is tested, not eyeballed — the same rule now applies to visual design. Every value below is buildable and verifiable, not descriptive.

| **Role** | **Hex** | **Use** | **Verified contrast** |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Ink | **#1F4B57** | Body text, headings, on white/light backgrounds | 9.54:1 on white — exceeds AAA |
| Action | **#0F7A6B** | Buttons, links, active states — white text on top | 5.23:1 white-on-accent — passes AA |
| Surface | **#F4F7F6** | Card and section backgrounds — never used for text itself | N/A — background only |

*Contrast ratios calculated against WCAG 2.1/2.2 formulae, not estimated. Every colour combination actually used in the build must still be tested against its real background with a contrast tool before release — this palette is the starting point, not a substitute for that gate.*

| **Element** | **Specification** |
| --- | --- |
| Typeface | Atkinson Hyperlegible — open-licence, purpose-built for reading clarity and accessibility, free to use. No licensing cost, no font-loading governance question. |
| Line length | 65–75 characters per line for body text — beyond that, readability drops measurably regardless of contrast or font choice. |
| Touch targets | Minimum 44×44px for every interactive element, per WCAG 2.2 target-size guidance — non-negotiable, not a nice-to-have. |
| Motion | Respects prefers-reduced-motion at the system level by default. No animation carries meaning on its own — anything animated must also work correctly with motion off. |
| Card surfaces | Flat colour only — Surface (#F4F7F6) or white. No semi-transparent overlays. This build previously learned that blended, semi-transparent surfaces pass automated contrast checks while failing real screen-reader and low-vision use — that lesson applies here from day one. |
| Focus states | Every interactive element has a visible focus outline distinct from hover state, tested against every background it appears on. |
| Imagery | Original diagrams or icons only. No stock photography of generic classrooms — it dates fast and says nothing specific about Weston or digital practice. |

# 11. Digital Practice Cards

| **Component** | **Specification** |
| --- | --- |
| Card title | Plain language, situation before tool name — e.g. "Show your thinking live with a visualiser." |
| Why this works | 40–60 words, plain language, no jargon that requires the Section 9 lens names to already be understood. |
| Try this | 3–5 steps, simplest viable approach first. |
| Routes | No-tech, low-tech, digital, AI-supported, where each genuinely applies — not forced onto every card. |
| Learning lenses | Accessibility and inclusion always present; UDL, trauma-informed and evidence-informed lenses only where genuinely relevant, per Section 9. |
| Framework alignment | Selected from the controlled vocabulary in Section 12's schema — never freeform text. |
| Source and review | Source area, last-reviewed date, review status. No named individual required for V1, per Section 12's trigger for change. |
| Downloads | Word guide or checklist, reusing existing Word-source material already produced across the Digital Skills Framework, Accessible by Design and Pedagogy Playbook. |

# 12. Source, Review and Contribution Model

| **Field** | **v6 position** |
| --- | --- |
| Named co-author | Not required for V1. Named Digital Lead review becomes a requirement, not an aspiration, for any card moving from Draft to Current status after V1 — this is a scheduled trigger, not a deferred hope. |
| Contributor model | Gemini drafts from approved source material; Graeme reviews for V1's five cards specifically; the Digital Lead trigger above applies from V1's close onward. |
| Review date | Every content item carries a last-reviewed date, displayed on the card. |
| Review status | Draft / Current / Needs review / Archived. |
| Survivability | Structured schemas, source areas, review status and versioning carry the product day to day. Named review from V1's close carries its long-term credibility and succession. |

# 13. Gemini + GitHub Build Model

| **Layer** | **Detail** |
| --- | --- |
| Source of truth | GitHub repo — code, schemas, content, version history, issues, handoff records. |
| Site Manager | A Gemini Gem maintaining the repo map, master schedule and issue log. |
| Builder sessions | Scoped to files provided in-session only; never assume a file exists or does not exist. |
| Automated validation — new in v6 | A committed script (Node + a JSON schema validator such as ajv) runs on every commit: validates every card and route against the schema files below, and hard-fails if any related_cards or suggested_cards ID does not resolve to a real file. Written AI rules describe intent; this enforces it. |
| Human review | Graeme reviews content quality and Weston-specific wording for V1; the Section 12 trigger applies from V1's close. |

# 14. Buildable Schema — Card Example (Controlled Vocabulary)

{
  "id": "reading-writing-support-immersive-reader",
  "version": "17/07/26 v1",
  "title": "Support reading and writing with Immersive Reader",
  "staff_situations": ["I need to support a learner with reading or writing barriers"],
  "underlying_needs": ["text access", "assistive technology", "reduce cognitive load of text"],
  "pyramid_layers": ["Digital Inclusion"],
  "tech_routes": ["digital", "AI-supported"],
  "digital_practices": ["Immersive Reader", "Read&Write", "dictation", "captions"],
  "learning_lenses": {
    "accessibility": ["text-to-speech", "line focus", "translation support"],
    "inclusive_practice": ["reduces barrier without singling out the learner"],
    "trauma_informed": ["avoids public exposure of a reading difficulty"]
  },
  "framework_alignment": ["WCAG: text alternatives", "Digital Skills Framework: assistive technology"],
  "source_area": "Digital Inclusion / Accessible by Design",
  "last_reviewed": "17/07/26",
  "review_status": "Draft",
  "downloads": ["word-guide", "checklist"],
  "related_cards": ["accessible-lesson-summary"]
}

*framework_alignment values must be drawn from a fixed, pre-approved list committed in the repo — WalkThrus is not on that list, and Gemini cannot add new entries to it without a human adding them to the schema file first.*

# 15. QA Gates

| **Gate** | **Builder must prove** |
| --- | --- |
| 1. Scope alignment | Every output passes the Section 3 test: tool, setting, template or accessible format — never a teaching, assessment or evidence judgement. |
| 2. No Jisc duplication | The Finder never asks users to rate confidence, skill or capability. |
| 3. Schema validation — new in v6 | Automated script passes on every commit: no broken card references, no framework_alignment values outside the approved list. |
| 4. Accessibility | WCAG 2.1/2.2 AA, keyboard navigation, focus states, contrast tested with a real tool, 200% zoom, touch targets 44×44px minimum. |
| 5. Design language | Palette, typeface and layout rules from Section 10 followed and verified, not approximated. |
| 6. Inclusion lenses | Every card includes accessibility and inclusion considerations at minimum. |
| 7. Framework safety | No trademarked or commercial framework formats referenced, alignment notes only. |
| 8. Handoff quality | Files changed, tests run, assumptions, unresolved risks, next action, provided for every session. |

# 16. Recommended V1 Build Scope

| **V1 slice** | **What it proves** |
| --- | --- |
| Digital Inclusion as the first full layer | Uses existing Accessible by Design and Learning Without Barriers content |
| Problem-to-Practice Finder, 7–9 refined situations | Situation-first routing with no confidence scoring, no Jisc duplication |
| Five Digital Practice Cards — all five prove the discovery model | Every card, not a sample of them, demonstrates tool-discovery-from-problem |
| Design language applied in full | Palette, typeface, contrast and layout rules built and tested, not approximated |
| Automated schema validation live from day one | Every commit checked, not just written rules relied on |
| Accessible design layer | Text size, contrast, reduced motion, 44px touch targets, visible focus states |

# 17. Approved Language for Stakeholders

| **Say this** | **Avoid this** |
| --- | --- |
| "It strengthens how staff deliver existing TLA practice through digital, accessible means." | "It improves teaching, learning and assessment." (implies ownership of the outcome, not the tooling) |
| "It starts from common staff situations and helps them discover useful digital possibilities." | "It tells teachers the best teaching strategy." |
| "Accessibility and inclusion are built into every card, not a separate section." | "Accessibility is one section staff can visit if needed." |
| "Framework references are alignment notes, not ownership claims." | "This is our Rosenshine platform." |
| "Design and accessibility are both tested against real tools before release." | "It looks clean and modern." (unverifiable, not buildable) |

# 18. Final Recommendation

*v6 is the version to build from. It keeps every governance and scope fix from v3 through v5, states the TLA-support position explicitly instead of managing it row by row, closes the two remaining risk points from the last review (the evidence-of-practical-work situation and the WalkThrus reference), replaces a written promise of schema discipline with an enforced one, and gives Gemini an accessible design specification precise enough to build against rather than interpret. The next real test remains the same as before: build the five Digital Inclusion cards, and check them against Section 3, Section 10 and the automated validator before deciding whether to scale beyond them.*